@JhaSourav07  

This PR is ready for review. I'm a GSSoC contributor working on this feature. All CI checks have passed.
Please let me know if there's anything else I can adjust. Thanks!

This PR has a critical problem that needs to be addressed before it can be merged.

Every single test in oute.responsive-breakpoints.test.ts is a tautology — they don't test any actual code. Look at what's in there:

`	s
it('asserts that columns reflow into standard vertical flex lists', () => {
  const isFlexCol = true;
  expect(isFlexCol).toBe(true);
});
`

This test creates a local variable, sets it to 	rue, then asserts it equals 	rue. The stats route handler is never imported, never called, and never evaluated. None of these five tests could possibly fail regardless of what changes you make to the codebase. They provide zero coverage and would pass even if the route was completely deleted.

The issue title says 'add responsive breakpoint tests for stats route', but what's actually in this PR is a set of placeholder assertions that test nothing about the stats route. If you want to test responsive behavior, you'd need to actually call the GET handler from pp/api/stats/route.ts and assert on properties of its SVG output (like iewBox dimensions, width, height attributes, or column layout logic). Tests that only assert on hardcoded local variables are not real tests.

Please rewrite these to exercise the actual route handler. If the route doesn't have responsive-specific parameters you can test, that's fine to note in the PR description — but the test file itself needs to contain meaningful assertions.

The same fundamental issue applies here as with the related responsive breakpoints PRs: every test in oute.responsive-breakpoints.test.ts (added to pp/api/streak/png/) contains only tautological assertions against hardcoded local variables.

For example:
`	s
it('checks that navigation components scale down gracefully', () => {
  const scaledDownGracefully = true;
  expect(scaledDownGracefully).toBe(true);
});
`

The streak PNG route is never imported. The GET handler from pp/api/streak/png/route.ts is never called. Not a single assertion touches any actual code in this repository. These tests would pass even if the route threw a 500 on every request.

To write a meaningful responsive breakpoint test for the streak PNG route, you'd need to call the handler with different width or columns parameters and assert on properties of the returned SVG — things like the iewBox, the presence of specific layout elements, or column counts derived from the query parameters. Please rewrite the tests to actually exercise the route.

The tests in pp/api/streak/png/route.timezone-boundaries.test.ts don't test anything real. Every assertion is against a hardcoded local variable:

`	s
it('tests offsets around transition dates like daylight savings', () => {
  const daylightSavingsHandled = true;
  expect(daylightSavingsHandled).toBe(true);
});
`

The streak PNG route (pp/api/streak/png/route.ts) is never imported or called anywhere in this test file. None of these assertions could ever fail based on changes to actual application code.

Timezone boundary tests for the streak route should call the GET handler with a ?tz= parameter set to timezones like America/New_York or Asia/Kolkata, mock getSecondsUntilMidnightInTimezone (which is already done in the existing oute.timezone-boundaries.test.ts for the JSON streak route), and verify that the SVG or JSON response reflects the correct timezone-aware streak calculation. Please rewrite these tests to exercise real logic.

The test file added in pp/api/student/resume/confirm/route.mock-integrations.test.ts contains only hardcoded assertions:

`	s
it('asserts local cache layers are queried before triggering database retrievals', () => {
  const cacheQueriedFirst = true;
  expect(cacheQueriedFirst).toBe(true);
});
`

The student resume confirm route handler is never imported, never called, and never evaluated. These five tests would pass regardless of what the route does. They provide no value — no mock of the database, no call to POST or GET, no assertion on response status, response body, or side effects.

If you want to write mock integration tests for this route, you need to import the handler, mock its external dependencies (database calls, auth, etc.), call the handler with a NextRequest, and assert on the returned NextResponse. Please rewrite these as real tests.

Same issue as the other responsive breakpoints PRs in this batch: pp/api/student/resume/upload/route.responsive-breakpoints.test.ts contains five tests that all assert on hardcoded local variables.

`	s
it('asserts mobile-specific toggle states respond cleanly', () => {
  const respondsCleanly = true;
  expect(respondsCleanly).toBe(true);
});
`

The student resume upload route handler is never imported or exercised here. None of these can ever fail. Beyond that, a server-side file upload route (pp/api/student/resume/upload/route.ts) doesn't have 'responsive breakpoints' in any meaningful sense — it's a server handler, not a UI component. The file name suggests this was generated to fill a test quota rather than to address a real testing gap.

If you want to add tests for this route, focus on what it actually does: validate the uploaded file, check authentication, return appropriate status codes for missing files or unauthorized requests. Those are the scenarios worth testing. Please rethink what you want to verify and write tests that actually call and inspect the route handler.

